{"attack": true, "case": "The mechanism's central assertion that acting on the best-tested theory is 'the only rational strategy to approximate truth, even without ... an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past' is question-begging. To label past severe testing as a rational guide to *approximating truth* (a forward-looking claim about which theory is closer to the truth) one must assume that surviving past tests is positively correlated with future truth-likeness. That correlation is precisely the inductive inference the target denies making. Relabeling it as a 'methodological rule' does not eliminate the inductive dependency; it conceals it. The claim of being non-inductive is therefore circular, not established.", "counterexample": ["Consider a domain in which a highly testable but false theory T survives all severe tests conducted so far, while a true but less testable theory U exists but has not been subjected to equally severe scrutiny. Popper's framework would rationally compel preference for T over U, since T has higher corroborating evidence. Yet T is farther from the truth than U, contradicting the claim that this strategy 'approximates truth.' The rule eliminates false theories but does not, without an inductive bridge, show it moves toward true ones."], "premise": "If acting on the best-tested theory is 'the only rational strategy to approximate truth,' this requires that past severe testing bears a positive evidential relationship to future truth-approximation — an inductive commitment — otherwise the connection between past performance and truth-approximation is unsupported.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "432fdd5217340a92b63e2c6cdf8de91c9efe7b6de41122353b48e60ace7722b3", "quote": "Acting on the best-tested theory is the only rational strategy to approximate truth, even without a guarantee of success or an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past."}], "successor_question": "What non-circular justification can Popper offer for the claim that surviving severe tests is truth-approximating rather than merely falsity-eliminating, that does not itself rely on the assumption that nature's future regularities resemble its past ones?"}